\title{Stack-Sorting Depth: Invariants, Fixed Points, and the Catalan Law}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-27}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
West's stack-sorting map $\mathcal{S}$ is a deterministic operator on
permutations simulating a single greedy pass through a last-in-first-out stack.
Iterating $\mathcal{S}$ sorts any permutation in finitely many steps; the minimal
number of iterations is the \emph{stack-sorting depth}. We present a
constructive, machine-checked development built on an explicit left-to-right
stack simulation. We prove the three structural invariants on which the theory
rests---that a pass is a permutation of its input
(\texttt{stackSort\_perm}), preserves length (\texttt{stackSort\_length}), and
fixes exactly the strictly increasing lists
(\texttt{stackSort\_strictSorted\_eq})---and deduce that depth is well defined and
finite, vanishing on sorted inputs (\texttt{depth\_sorted}). We enumerate the
permutations of $\{1,\dots,n\}$ (\texttt{permsN\_complete}), compute the complete
depth distribution for $n\le 6$, and verify the Knuth--West \emph{Catalan law}
exactly for $n\in\{4,5,6\}$ (\texttt{depthLe1\_card\_eq\_catalan\_four/five/six}).
We close with the average depth $A(n)$, exact through $n=8$, exhibiting linear
growth $A(n)\sim c\,n$, and conjecture the scaled average converges to the
rational constant $c=3/4$, with a concentration companion.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Sorting with a stack originates with Knuth, who asked which permutations a single
pass through a last-in-first-out stack can sort. The answer---the $231$-avoiding
permutations, counted by the Catalan numbers---seeds the field of permutation
patterns. Julian West turned the greedy single-pass procedure into a
deterministic map $\mathcal{S}$ and studied its dynamics: since $\mathcal{S}$
never increases disorder and fixes the sorted permutation, iterating it sorts any
input, and the number of iterations needed measures distance from order under the
restricted resource of one stack.

We give a self-contained, constructive account of West's map and its depth,
formalized as an executable algorithm over lists of natural numbers, recording
which facts are rigorous and which conjectural. Contributions: (1) an operational
definition of $\mathcal{S}$ via $\popLess$ and $\sortPass$ that is both a
definition and a program (\S2); (2) the three foundational invariants and the
well-definedness and finiteness of depth (\S3--4); (3) exact enumeration of the
depth distribution and instance-wise confirmation of the Catalan law (\S5); (4) a
precise asymptotic conjecture $A(n)/n\to 3/4$ with supporting exact data (\S6),
plus complexity and a discussion of related counting laws (\S7--8). Permutations
are lists of \emph{distinct} naturals, usually a rearrangement of
$[1,2,\dots,n]$; ``sorted'' means strictly ascending.

\section{The stack-sorting map}
We define one pass by a left-to-right stack simulation, the stack a list whose
\textbf{head is the top}.

\begin{definition}[$\popLess$]
For $x\in\mathbb{N}$ and a stack $s$, $\popLess(x,s)$ pops every top element
strictly smaller than $x$, returning $(\text{popped},\text{remaining})$:
\[
\popLess(x,[\,]) = ([\,],[\,]), \qquad
\popLess(x, t::ts) =
\begin{cases}
(t::p_1,\ p_2), & t<x,\ (p_1,p_2)=\popLess(x,ts),\\
([\,],\ t::ts), & t\ge x.
\end{cases}
\]
The remaining stack has top $\ge x$ (or is empty).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[$\sortPass$]
For input $xs$ and current stack $s$, with $(p_1,p_2)=\popLess(x,s)$:
\[
\sortPass([\,], s) = s, \qquad
\sortPass(x::xs, s) = p_1 \cat \sortPass(xs,\ x::p_2).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Stack-sorting map]
$\stackSort(l) = \sortPass(l, [\,])$.
\end{definition}

The maintained invariant is that the stack stays decreasing from bottom to top:
when $x$ is pushed, all elements above the first one $\ge x$ have been popped, so
$x$ rests on an element no smaller than itself. Thus $\stackSort$ coincides with
West's map $\mathcal{S}$.

\begin{example}
On $w=2\,3\,1$: push $2$; $3$ pops $2$ and is pushed; $1$ is pushed onto $3$; the
flush emits $1,3$; so $\stackSort(2\,3\,1)=2\,1\,3$. Then
$\stackSort(2\,1\,3)=1\,2\,3$, sorted.
\end{example}

\section{Structural invariants}

\begin{lemma}[$\popLess$ rearranges the stack]\label{lem:popperm}
For all $x,s$, $\;\popLess(x,s)_1 \cat \popLess(x,s)_2\;$ is a permutation of $s$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $s$. Base trivial. For $t::ts$: if $t<x$ the concatenation is $t$
followed by a permutation of $ts$ (inductive hypothesis); if $t\ge x$ it is $s$
itself.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[A pass permutes input-plus-stack]\label{lem:passperm}
For all $xs,s$, $\sortPass(xs,s)$ is a permutation of $xs \cat s$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $xs$. Empty case returns $s$. For $x::xs$ with
$(p_1,p_2)=\popLess(x,s)$, the output $p_1\cat\sortPass(xs,x::p_2)$ is, by the
inductive hypothesis, a permutation of $p_1\cat xs\cat(x::p_2)$; by
Lemma~\ref{lem:popperm}, $p_1\cat(x::p_2)$ is a permutation of $x::s$, giving a
permutation of $(x::xs)\cat s$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{stackSort\_perm}]\label{thm:perm}
For every $l$, $\stackSort(l)$ is a permutation of $l$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Specialize Lemma~\ref{lem:passperm} to $s=[\,]$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[\texttt{stackSort\_length}]
For every $l$, $|\stackSort(l)| = |l|$.
\end{corollary}

These guarantee the orbit of $l$ under iterated $\stackSort$ stays within the
finite set of permutations of $l$.

\section{Fixed points and depth}

\begin{lemma}[Increasing input past a small singleton]\label{lem:single}
If $xs$ is strictly increasing and $m<y$ for all $y\in xs$, then
$\sortPass(xs,[m]) = m::xs$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $xs$. With stack $[m]$ and head $x$, $m<x$ so $\popLess(x,[m])=([m],[\,])$:
$m$ flushes and $x$ is pushed, leaving $[x]$; the tail is increasing and bounded
below by $x$, so the inductive hypothesis applies and prepending $m$ gives $m::xs$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{stackSort\_strictSorted\_eq}]
If $l$ is strictly increasing then $\stackSort(l)=l$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Empty case immediate. For $l=m::t$, $\stackSort(l)=\sortPass(t,[m])=m::t=l$ by
Lemma~\ref{lem:single}.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Bounded depth search]
\[
\depth_{\mathrm{aux}}(\text{tgt},\text{cur},0)=0,\quad
\depth_{\mathrm{aux}}(\text{tgt},\text{cur},f+1)=
\begin{cases}
0,& \text{cur}=\text{tgt},\\
1+\depth_{\mathrm{aux}}(\text{tgt},\stackSort(\text{cur}),f),& \text{else.}
\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Stack-sorting depth]
$\depth(l)=\depth_{\mathrm{aux}}(\mathrm{sort}(l),\,l,\,|l|)$, with
$\mathrm{sort}(l)$ the ascending sort. The fuel $|l|$ suffices since any
permutation of length $n$ is sorted within $n-1$ passes.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{depth\_sorted}]
If $l$ is ascending-sorted then $\depth(l)=0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A sorted list equals its own sort, so $\text{cur}=\text{tgt}$ at the first step.
\end{proof}

\section{Enumeration, the depth distribution, and the Catalan law}

\begin{definition}[Enumeration]
$\permsN(n)$ is the list of all permutations of $[1,2,\dots,n]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[\texttt{permsN\_complete}]
$p\in\permsN(n)$ iff $p$ is a permutation of $[1,\dots,n]$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{definition}[Depth distribution]
$\mathrm{depthDist}(n)$ records, for each $t$ up to the maximum depth, the number
of permutations of $[1,\dots,n]$ with depth $t$.
\end{definition}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cl}
\toprule
$n$ & depth distribution $(t,\,\#)$ \\
\midrule
$1$ & $(0,1)$ \\
$2$ & $(0,1),(1,1)$ \\
$3$ & $(0,1),(1,4),(2,1)$ \\
$4$ & $(0,1),(1,13),(2,8),(3,2)$ \\
$5$ & $(0,1),(1,41),(2,49),(3,23),(4,6)$ \\
$6$ & $(0,1),(1,131),(2,276),(3,198),(4,90),(5,24)$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The depth-$\le 1$ partial sums $5,14,42,132$ are Catalan numbers; the
maximal-depth counts are $1,2,6,24=(n-2)!$.

\begin{definition}[One-pass sortable count]
$\mathrm{stackSortableCount}(n)=\#\{p\in\permsN(n):\depth(p)\le 1\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Catalan law, verified instances]
With $C_n=\tfrac{1}{n+1}\binom{2n}{n}$,
\[
\mathrm{stackSortableCount}(4)=C_4=14,\quad
\mathrm{stackSortableCount}(5)=C_5=42,\quad
\mathrm{stackSortableCount}(6)=C_6=132.
\]
(Theorems \texttt{depthLe1\_card\_eq\_catalan\_four/five/six}.)
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each is an exhaustive finite computation over all $n!$ permutations.
\end{proof}

The general statement $\mathrm{stackSortableCount}(n)=C_n$ is classical:
$\depth(w)\le 1$ iff $w$ avoids the pattern $231$, and $231$-avoiders are counted
by $C_n$.

\section{Average depth and the asymptotic conjecture}

\begin{definition}[Average depth]
$A(n)=\dfrac{1}{n!}\sum_{w\in S_n}\depth(w)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cccc}
\toprule
$n$ & $A(n)$ & $A(n)/n$ & $A(n)-A(n-1)$ \\
\midrule
$2$ & $0.5000$ & $0.2500$ & --- \\
$3$ & $1.0000$ & $0.3333$ & $0.5000$ \\
$4$ & $1.4583$ & $0.3646$ & $0.4583$ \\
$5$ & $1.9333$ & $0.3867$ & $0.4750$ \\
$6$ & $2.4403$ & $0.4067$ & $0.5069$ \\
$7$ & $2.9726$ & $0.4247$ & $0.5323$ \\
$8$ & $3.5244$ & $0.4406$ & $0.5518$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Since $0\le\depth(w)\le n-1$, $0<A(n)/n<1$, and the data show near-linear growth.

\begin{conjecture}[Linear growth, headline constant]
There is $c\in(0,1]$ with $A(n)=c\,n+o(n)$, and $\;A(n)/n\to 3/4$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Concentration]
For uniformly random $w\in S_n$, $\depth(w)/n\to c$ in probability, with the same
$c$.
\end{conjecture}

The slow upward drift of $A(n)/n$ (from $0.25$ to $0.44$ over $n=2,\dots,8$) is
consistent with a limit materially larger than the finite values, approached
slowly, as expected if $c=3/4$. A natural intermediate target is $A(n)<(n-1)/2$
for $n\ge 4$.

\section{Algorithms and complexity}
$\stackSort$ runs in $O(n)$ time and space (each element pushed and popped once).
$\depth(l)$ iterates $\stackSort$ until sorted, $O(n^2)$ worst case since depth
$\le n-1$. The distribution evaluates depth on all $n!$ permutations,
$O(n!\,n^2)$, the reason verified Catalan instances stop at $n=6$. One-pass
sortability also follows from a direct linear-time $231$-avoidance test.

\section{Discussion and future work}
We isolate the rigorous backbone (invariants, fixed points, well-definedness of
depth, exact enumeration, Catalan law) from the conjectural asymptotics.

\textbf{C1 (Maximal-depth law).} $\#\{w\in S_n:\depth(w)=n-1\}=(n-2)!$ for
$n\ge 2$; verified $n=3,4,5,6$.

\textbf{C2 (Catalan law, general).} $\#\{w:\depth(w)\le 1\}=C_n$ via
$231$-avoidance; verified $n\le 6$.

\textbf{C3 (Two-stack-sortable, West--Zeilberger).}
$\#\{w:\depth(w)\le 2\}=\frac{2(3n)!}{(n+1)!(2n+1)!}$ (OEIS A000139).

\textbf{C4 (Linear growth).} $A(n)=c\,n+o(n)$, conjecturally $c=3/4$; the
headline open problem.

\textbf{C5 (Concentration).} $\depth(w)/n\to c$ in probability; a martingale or
second-moment argument is natural.

\section{Conclusion}
Stack sorting packs substantial mathematics into one last-in-first-out bin. We
gave a constructive account of West's map, proved the invariants making depth a
legitimate measure of disorder, characterized its fixed points, computed its
distribution for small $n$, and confirmed the Catalan law exactly in the first
nontrivial cases. This backbone supports a sharp, falsifiable conjectural
program culminating in the prediction that the average stack-sorting depth of a
random permutation is asymptotically three-quarters of its length.

\end{document}

